.env
# The publications in sources/ ARE committed. Every citation the assistant
# emits links to one, and a clone without them has attribution without
# evidence, which is the thing this corpus exists to avoid.

# Frontend build output (frontend/.gitignore also covers these)
node_modules/
.next/

# Scratch
*.log
frontend/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
eval/last-run.json

# From the Supabase backend branch
logs
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
lerna-debug.log*
node_modules
dist
dist-ssr
*.local
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea
.DS_Store
*.suo
*.ntvs*
*.njsproj
*.sln
*.sw?
venv/
*.py[cod]
uploads/

# Uploaded scans are user data, not source
backend/uploads/

# Survey output. The artefacts an operator or the dashboard actually reads are
# committed, so a fresh clone demos with no processing step: export.json,
# actions.csv, map.html and the two manifests, about 3 MB across three surveys.
#
# Tiles and the simulated strip are not. They are regenerable from the source
# imagery in samples/, they are 10 MB of the 13, and nothing at runtime opens
# them: map.html embeds its own imagery as a data URI and the API never reads a
# tile. They are needed only to REBUILD a map, which run_survey.py does.
data/surveys/*/tiles/
data/surveys/*/simulated_survey/

# ONNX exports are rebuilt from models/*.pt in seconds (tools/export_onnx.py),
# and each export stamps its date into the file, so committing them would add
# a new ~57 MB blob to history on every re-export. See docs/EDGE.md.
models/*.onnx

# Strip images the importer writes beside a survey: regenerable, and a GeoTIFF mosaic is 100 MB.
data/surveys/*/strips/
applications/

# One developer's local state, never the team's.
data/deepecho.db*
data/uploads/
data/survey_uploads/
data/surveys/mission-*/
rag_assistant/eval/last-run.json
# ONNX exports and TensorRT caches are built per machine (tools/export_onnx.py).
survey_hazard_map/models/**/*.onnx
survey_hazard_map/models/**/trt_cache/
